 5. 💉 Trump dives into vaccine minefield 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump holds up a printed Truth Social post during an Oval Office meeting to sign an executive order on vaccines yesterday. Photo: Jacquelyn Martin/AP President Trump's call for a controversial overhaul of the childhood immunization schedule yesterday puts high-risk vaccine policy changes front and center as the midterm elections approach, Axios' Caitlin Owens writes. 

 Why it matters: The executive order — which comes in the middle of measles and other infectious disease outbreaks — could further erode trust in vaccines and public health. 🔬 Zoom in: Trump signed an executive order calling for a slimmed-down childhood vaccine schedule and breaking the measles-mumps-rubella vaccine into three separately administered shots.

 It also advises states to review federal vaccine recommendations and update school enrollment requirements. At an Oval Office signing event, Trump suggested the MMR vaccine could be "quite lethal." When pressed, he acknowledged he had no evidence, adding that "some people that say it is that way." Via X 🥊 Reality check: The overwhelming majority of scientists and public health experts say the benefits of vaccines greatly outweigh the risks, and though the shots do have rare side effects, no link to autism has been found.

