 8. 🏎️ 1 for the road: D.C.&#x27;s IndyCar architect 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bud Denker speaks at an event outside the West Wing with President Trump last month. Photo: Andrew Harnik/Getty Images Gabe Hiatt — author of our new Axios D.C. PM newsletter — writes in his inaugural issue yesterday: 

 It typically takes two and a half years to stage a race through city streets. For the Freedom 250 Grand Prix on the National Mall (Aug. 22-23) , race chairman Bud Denker had seven months.

 Why it matters: To bring IndyCar onto D.C. streets lined with museums and monuments, the race chairman had to become a Washington insider, navigating Congress and federal agencies. 🏁 Zoom in: Manhole covers have to be welded shut because the downforce from race cars will hoover them up.

 Denker says the weekend of racing required 1,300 pages of permits. The best view of the race: FTC headquarters, where spotters will be perched on the roof to radio advice to their drivers. 🟩 What we're watching: Denker says Trump could be the one waving the green flag to start the race.

